"Almost everything known about Zhuangzis life fits into a paragraph. Sima Qian, writing about two centuries later, records that he was a man of Meng, that his name was Zhou, and that he once held a post at a place called the Lacquer Garden. He lived at the time of King Hui of Liang and King Xuan of Qi, which puts him in the second half of the fourth century BCE, the most violent period of the Warring States. The historian adds that there was nothing his learning did not look into, that his writing ran to more than a hundred thousand characters, and that it was mostly made of parables. His words, says Sima Qian, were like a flood that spread wherever it pleased, and for that reason no king or minister could make use of him.": "e3a6da52868b",
"Dao (the Way)": "787154af1ae7",
"One story is told in two versions. In Sima Qians, King Wei of Chu sent messengers with rich gifts and the offer of the post of chief minister. Zhuangzi laughed and asked whether they had seen the ox that is fattened for the great sacrifice. It is fed for years and dressed in embroidered cloth, and on the day it is led into the temple it would gladly change places with a lonely piglet. Go away, and do not defile me. In the version in the Zhuangzi itself he is fishing and does not turn his head. He has heard that Chu keeps a sacred tortoise, three thousand years dead, wrapped in cloth in the ancestral hall. Would that tortoise prefer to be dead with its bones honored, or alive and dragging its tail in the mud? Alive in the mud, the officials admit. Then go. I will drag my tail in the mud.": "ec8d58addbe4",
"The Zhuangzi that we read was put into its present shape around 300 CE by the commentator Guo Xiang, who cut a version in fifty-two chapters down to thirty-three and arranged them in three groups. Scholars generally accept the seven Inner Chapters as the work of Zhuang Zhou, or very close to him. The fifteen Outer Chapters and eleven Miscellaneous Chapters were written by followers and sympathizers over the next two centuries, and they vary in outlook and quality. This matters for the stories told here. The butterfly dream and Cook Ding are in the Inner Chapters. The fish on the Hao, the tortoise in the mud, the drumming on the pot, Huizis grave, and the deathbed are all in the later sections. They are what his school remembered or invented about him, and we cannot tell which. The figure they draw is so consistent, and so unlike anyone else in ancient literature, that readers have always felt they were meeting a real person. It is safer to say that they are meeting a character whom a real person made possible.": "ce5c31d6eea4",
"The first serious criticism came within a generation of his death. The Confucian philosopher Xunzi listed the partial views of his predecessors, each of whom had seen one corner of the truth and taken it for the whole, and gave Zhuangzi one line: he was blinded by Heaven and did not know the human. Zhuangzi saw so clearly how small human distinctions look from the standpoint of nature that he lost sight of what those distinctions are for. People must decide how to raise children, share food, and restrain the violent, and a state at war cannot be governed by a man who is dragging his tail in the mud. Refusing office is a clean choice for one person. It is no answer for a society.": "a98c7fb36094",
"The other person who appears again and again in the book is Hui Shi, called Huizi, the leading logician of the age and for a time chief minister of Liang. The two disagreed about everything. When Zhuangzi came to visit, Huizi was told that he had come to take his post, and had the city searched for three days. Zhuangzi walked in and told him about a southern bird that rests only on the parasol tree and drinks only from sweet springs. An owl that had got hold of a rotten rat looked up as it flew over and screeched to protect its prize. Are you screeching at me over your state of Liang? The debate on the bridge over the Hao, about whether one can know that fish are happy, is the most famous of their exchanges.": "5ea170bd54fb",
"The philosophers of Zhuangzis day, Confucians and Mohists above all, argued about which course of action is right and which is wrong. The second chapter of his book, On Making Things Equal, steps back from the quarrel. Each side, he observes, calls right what the other calls wrong, and each can see the others blindness without seeing its own. Words such as this and that depend on where the speaker stands, and he suggests that right and wrong work in the same way. That comes out of this, and this depends on that. A keeper told his monkeys they would get three nuts in the morning and four at night, and they were furious. He offered four in the morning and three at night, and they were delighted. Nothing had changed except their joy and anger. The sage, says Zhuangzi, does not take up a position among the positions. He stands at the hinge of the Way, the still point from which every view can be seen to be one view.": "b5e3a33f8d25",
"The resemblances are real. Zhuangzis I lost myself and his sitting and forgetting, in which one lets the limbs fall away and dismisses the intellect, would be recognized by any meditator, and his calm before death is what the Buddhist texts call equanimity. The two part company on what the trouble is. For Zhuangzi the endless transformation of things is to be welcomed: one rides the changes, and death is one more turn of the seasons. For the Buddha, being carried from birth to birth by causes one does not understand is the very thing from which liberation is sought. Zhuangzi offers no analysis of why we suffer and no graded path, and compassion for other beings is not among his themes: his fish do best when they can forget one another. The Buddha taught for forty-five years because he thought that the stranded fish should be helped back to the water (Dharma and the West, D24).": "e3b81e8b9cd5",
"The second objection is logical. If every judgment of right and wrong is only a view from somewhere, then so is that judgment, and Zhuangzi has no ground for preferring his outlook to the Confucian one. His defenders reply that he never says all views are equally good. The clumsy cook who hacks at the bone and changes his knife every month is plainly worse at living than Cook Ding. What Zhuangzi denies is that any fixed set of words can capture the difference in advance. He writes in stories and jokes because a doctrine would be one more position. His school understood this: the last chapter of the book describes his writing as absurd talk and extravagant words.": "439e5a586472",
"This could be a recipe for paralysis, and Zhuangzi means it as a release. His heroes are people who do something supremely well without deliberating: a wheelwright, a swimmer, a catcher of cicadas, and above all Cook Ding, who cuts up an ox for his lord with the grace of a dance. Asked about his skill, the cook says that what he cares for is the Way, which goes beyond skill. After nineteen years he no longer sees the ox with his eyes. His blade, which has no thickness, moves through the spaces that are already there in the joints, and it is as sharp as on the day it left the whetstone. The lord replies that he has learned from this how to care for life. To stop forcing ones categories on things and to follow their grain is what the tradition calls wuwei, action without strain. The books first chapter is titled Wandering Far and Free.": "847c72012247",
"When Buddhist texts reached China in the first centuries of the common era, the only native vocabulary subtle enough to translate them was that of Laozi and Zhuangzi. Emptiness was first rendered by the Daoist word for nothingness, and nirvana by wuwei. The practice was later called geyi, matching concepts, and it took Chinese monks some three hundred years to see how much it distorted. But the meeting left a permanent mark. Sengzhao, the first great Chinese exponent of the Middle Way, was formed by reading Zhuangzi before he ever saw a sutra, and Chan Buddhism owes to Zhuangzi much of its manner: the humor, the craftsmen and cooks as teachers, and the distrust of anything that can be put into words.": "ec2838a1ecd0",
"When Zhuangzis wife died, Huizi came to offer condolences and found him sitting with his legs sprawled, drumming on a pot and singing. This was too much. She had lived with him, raised his children, and grown old. Zhuangzi answered that when she first died he had grieved like anyone. Then he had looked back to her beginning, to the time before she had life, or form, or even the breath that takes form. Out of some dim confusion there came breath, then form, then life, and now another change had brought death. It was like the passage of the four seasons. She is lying at rest in a great chamber. If I were to follow after her sobbing, I would show that I do not understand what is ordained. So I stopped.": "c81d7fc9b058",
"When he himself was dying, his students planned a grand funeral. He told them that heaven and earth would be his inner and outer coffins, the sun and moon his jade discs, the stars his pearls, and the ten thousand things his funeral gifts. What could they add? They said they were afraid the crows and kites would eat him. Above ground I will be eaten by crows and kites, and below ground by ants and crickets. To rob the one and give to the other: how partial that would be!": "93f40748589f",
"Years later, passing Huizis grave, Zhuangzi told his companions a story. A plasterer of Ying used to let a speck of plaster as thin as a flys wing sit on the tip of his nose, and have his friend the carpenter slice it off with an axe. The axe whirled like the wind and the nose was never touched. A lord heard of it and asked the carpenter to do it for him. I could once do it, said the carpenter, but my partner has long been dead. Zhuangzi went on: Since the Master died, I have had no one to use as my partner. I have no one to talk to. It is the best thing ever said about what a thinker owes to an opponent.": "87a0702c2c5b",
